/*
  Welcome to GD++!
  GD++ is a Domain Specific Language (DSL) that compiles to regular C++.
  Its one goal: easy C++ development for the Godot engine, without boilerplate.
  This file shows the basic syntax and features of GD++.

  This text is a multi-line comment. It starts with /* and ends with the *matching* */.
  So this line is still inside the comment.
  This works like in Rust, not like in C++.
*/

// This is a single-line comment.
// This tutorial uses them a lot.

// Part I. Lexical scopes.

// GD++ has 4 lexical scopes (plus helper scopes such as comments and strings):
// 1. Class declaration scope.
//    The syntax looks a lot like GDScript, but it is *not* GDScript.
//    (In the same way, GDScript looks like Python but is not Python.)
//    Unlike GDScript, GD++ is not whitespace sensitive: blocks use braces, not indentation.
// 2. Property declaration scope.
//    A small scope. It only groups a property's declaration, getter and setter.
// 3. Enum declaration scope.
//    A list of enum names, each with an optional integer value.
//    Separate names with spaces, newlines or commas.
// 4. C++ implementation scope.
//    Any C++ code is allowed here, with a few small changes.
//    The main one: GD++ still parses comments here and strips them from the generated C++.
//    So multi-line comments nest here too, as described above.

// Part II. Classes.

// A file may have one file-level class. If present, it goes at the very top of the file:

class_name MyNode
extends Node3D  // Without `extends`, the class inherits from RefCounted.
// We are now in the class declaration scope.

// A file can also have inline classes:
class MyNode1 {
  extends Node
  // We are in the class declaration scope here too.
}

class MyRefCounted1 {
  // Inherits from RefCounted by default, like in GDScript.
}

// File-level and inline classes work the same. Pick whichever you like.
// The generated file puts all classes on the same level.
// GD++ has no nested classes.

// A file without a file-level class has a limited class declaration scope.
// It only allows inline entities: classes, enums and externs (see below).
// This file has a file-level class, so here we have the full class declaration scope.

// Part III. Embedding C++.

// GD++ lets you embed any C++ code anywhere in the file.
// This is very powerful, but use it rarely: it brings back boilerplate.
// There are two keywords, decl and impl. You can combine them as decl impl.

decl {
  // decl puts this C++ code inside the class definition, in the header file.
  // To put code outside the class and outside the godot:: namespace, use decl before class_name.
  // (Only decl, impl and decl impl may appear before class_name.)
  void private_function();
}

impl {
  // impl puts this C++ code in the source file, inside the godot:: namespace.
  // To put code outside the godot:: namespace, use impl before class_name.
  void This::private_function() {
    // `This` is the name of the current class, like Self in Rust.
    // GD++ emits a #define for it (and an #undef at the end of the file).
    if (Engine::is_editor_hint()) return;

    // `gd` is another shorthand: an alias for UtilityFunctions.
    // These are Godot's "global scope" functions.
    gd::print("Hello, world!");
  }
}

// decl impl puts one block in both the header and the source file:

decl impl {
  #define FOO 42
  // Don't forget to #undef at the end of the file!
}

// decl code goes in the private section of the class.
// To make it public or protected, add an access specifier inside the decl block:

decl {
 public:
  void public_function();  // Visible to all C++ classes, but not exposed to Godot.
}

// Part IV. Godot types.

// GD++ uses Godot's type system.
// Each Godot type maps to one canonical C++ type:
// - bool -> bool
// - int -> int64_t
// - float -> float64_t (a typedef for double, provided by GD++)
// - Non-object types, e.g. Vector3 -> the C++ struct with the same name, from the Godot C++ bindings.
// - Non-refcounted objects, e.g. Node -> a pointer: Node*
// - Refcounted objects, e.g. Resource -> a reference: Ref<Resource>
// - GD++ enums, e.g. MyEnum -> a generated C++ enum class, so MyEnum::VALUE_1 etc. are available (see Part V).
// - Non-refcounted externs -> ExtPtr<MyExtern> (see Part XII).
// - Refcounted externs -> ExtRef<MyExtern> (see Part XII).

// Part V. Constants and enums.

// GDExtension can only expose integer constants to Godot, so GD++ only supports those.
// Define a constant like this:

enum THE_ANSWER = 42

// GD++ has no const keyword.

// GD++ enums differ a bit from C++, Godot and GDScript enums.
// In GD++, an enum is a global type. For example:

enum Suit {
  DIAMONDS
  CLUBS
  SPADES
  HEARTS
}

enum Rank {
  JACK = 0
  QUEEN
  KING
}

// These are inline enums. Every file in the GD++ package can use them.
// In Godot, every enum belongs to a class, so GD++ must attach each enum to a class.
// GD++ copies the enum into every class that uses it in its API:
// in a variable, function argument, return value, or signal argument.
// The file-level class also exports the inline enums defined in its file.

// The exported values are named SUIT_DIAMONDS, SUIT_CLUBS, etc.
// (GD++ converts PascalCase to UPPER_SNAKE_CASE automatically.)

// To force a class to export an enum, use import. To stop it from exporting one, use noimport:
import Suit  // Redundant, since this file already defines Suit. It is only a demonstration.
noimport Rank  // This class won't export Rank. Other classes that use or import Rank may still export it.

// Like class_name, enum_name turns the whole file into an enum and enters the enum scope.
// It is rarely used, but exists for consistency. For example, a whole file could be:
// > enum_name Foo
// > FOO1
// > FOO2
// That file exports no classes to Godot, but other GD++ files can use the enum.

// Part VI. Functions.

// The function syntax is inspired by GDScript:

func greet_user(name: String) -> void {
  // C++ implementation.
  gd::print("Hello ", name, "!");
}

// An argument or return value without a type is a Variant.

// Arguments can have default values. Like initial values of variables (see Part IX), a default value is a
// C++ expression, or a block of C++ code that returns the value:

func greet_users(count: int = 1, greeting: String = {
  return String("Hello");
}) -> void {
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    gd::print(greeting, "!");
  }
}

// Arguments with default values must come last.
// Godot computes each default value once, when it registers the class.
// So a default value can't use the object (`this`) or the other arguments.

// GD++ makes functions public in C++ and registers them with Godot in the generated _bind_methods.
// All types in a signature are Godot types. In C++ code, they become their canonical C++ types (see Part IV).

// The @virtual attribute defines a function that scripts can override:

@virtual
func _foo() -> int {
  return 0;
}

@virtual
func _bar() -> int  // Also OK: GD++ generates a body that returns the type's default value (here, 0).

func foo_plus_bar() -> int {
  return _foo() + _bar();
}

// A GDScript can then override them, e.g.
// func _foo() -> int:
//   return 42

// To override an engine virtual function, use @override instead:
@override
func _ready() -> void {
  // Caveat: the signature must match *exactly*.
  // E.g. without `-> void` the return type would be Variant, which does not match _ready, so compilation fails.
  gd::print("My node is ready.");
}

// The @const attribute makes a function const:

decl {
  int64_t my_value_;
}

@const
func get_my_value() -> int {
  return my_value;
}

// The @static attribute makes a function static:

@static
func my_static_func() -> void {
  gd::print("Hello!");
}

// The @rpc attribute makes a function callable over the network, as in GDScript:

@rpc("any_peer", "call_local", "reliable")
func take_damage(amount: int) -> void {
  gd::print("Took ", amount, " damage.");
}

// Its arguments are optional, as in GDScript: the mode ("authority" or "any_peer"), the sync ("call_remote" or
// "call_local"), the transfer mode ("unreliable", "unreliable_ordered" or "reliable"), and last the channel number.
// The class must extend Node. Call the function remotely like this:

func hurt_everyone(peer: int) -> void {
  rpc take_damage(10);  // On every peer. Like take_damage.rpc(10) in GDScript.
  rpc_id(peer) take_damage(10);  // On one peer. Like take_damage.rpc_id(peer, 10).
  take_damage(10);  // Locally, as a plain call.
}

// Like `emit`, `rpc` makes remote calls look different from local ones. Both return an Error.
// A remote call can't return a value, so any return value of an @rpc function is lost.

// You can combine attributes where it makes sense, e.g. @virtual @const.

// Part VII. Constructors and destructors.

// A class has at most one constructor and at most one destructor.
// Neither takes arguments. Syntax:

ctor {
  gd::print("This message shows up every time a MyNode is created.");
}

dtor {
  gd::print("This message shows up every time a MyNode is deleted.");
}

// Part VIII. Signals.

// The signal syntax is inspired by GDScript:

signal something_happened(a: int)
signal something_else_happened

// Emit them from C++ code like this:

func emit_both_signals() -> void {
  emit something_happened(42);
  emit something_else_happened();
}

// GD++ generates a function for each signal. It emits the signal and returns a [[nodiscard]] struct.
// `emit` marks that value as used, so the compiler does not warn.
// This is on purpose: emitting a signal should look different from calling a function.

// Part IX. Variables.

// There are two kinds of variables: simple variables and properties.
// Under the hood they are the same. Only the syntax differs.

var simple_var: int  // Initialized to 0 by default.
var another_simple_var: int = 5  // Initialized to 5.

// The initial value can be a C++ expression, or a block of C++ code that returns the value:

@onready
var child_node_1: Node3D = get_node<Node3D>("MyNode1")

@onready
var child_node_2: Node3D = {
  Node3D* res = memnew(Node3D);
  add_child(res);
  return res;
}

// @onready is optional. It runs the initialization at the start of _ready instead of in the constructor.

// In C++, a simple variable is a field with the same name and the canonical C++ type.
// E.g. simple_var is an int64_t field of MyNode.
// GD++ exposes it to Godot through a generated getter and setter.

// A property is a variable with a custom getter and setter.
// The getter is required, since Godot doesn't support set-only properties. Without a setter it is get-only.

// Godot can never set this property to a negative number.
var my_property: int {
  decl {
    // Goes in the private section of the class.
    int64_t my_property = 0;
  }
  get {
    // The generated public method is: int64_t get_my_property() const;
    return my_property;
  }
  set(val) {
    // The generated public method is: void set_my_property(int64_t val);
    if (val < 0) val = 0;
    my_property = val;
  }
}

// Part X. Includes.

// In GD++ you don't write #include lines.
// At compile time, GD++ scans the available C++ files and builds a table of includes.
// The table covers both Godot/GDExtension classes and your own classes.
// GD++ then includes every type that is spelled out anywhere in the code: in the public API or in C++ code.

// In other words: to include a class, just use it.

// In rare cases this fails, e.g. when you inspect an object without naming its type in code.
// To force an include, use:

import MeshInstance3D  // Forces the #include of the right header.

// To prevent an include (this almost always breaks compilation, but you are allowed to do it):

noimport MeshInstance2D

// As a last resort, write decl { #include <...> } before class_name. Avoid this if at all possible.

// Part XI. Annotations.

// Annotations are structured data attached to the syntax tree.
// By convention, an annotation goes right before the declaration it belongs to.
// The syntax is @annotation(param1, ...), or just @annotation when there are no params.
// For example:

@export_range(0, 100, 1)
var my_range_var: int

// Documentation comments are a special kind of annotation with their own syntax.
// Like normal comments, they come in single-line and multi-line forms:

/// This is a variable.
var some_var: int

/**
  This function has a
  long
  description.
*/
func foo() -> void {}

// Part XII. Externs.

// A GD++ package can't depend on another package at compile time.
// But sometimes you need a dependency at runtime. Externs make this easy.
// Here we use an inline extern. There is also a file-level extern_name, like class_name and enum_name.

extern Terrain {
  extends Node
  // Externs have only declarations: *NO* function bodies, property bodies, or initial values.
  @const func foo() -> void
  @deferred func bar() -> void
  @rpc func qux() -> void
  var baz: int
  signal done(a: bool, b: int)
}

// In C++ and in the public Godot API, an extern is just an object of its base class.
// E.g. an ExtPtr<Terrain> holds a plain Node* under the hood.

// You can expose an extern, e.g.:
@export var my_extern: Terrain

// In Godot, my_extern has the type Node. But the generated property bindings mention the type "Terrain".
// So the Godot editor's inspector only lets you assign a "Terrain" node to it.

// GD++ generates wrappers, so you can use externs like regular types.
// But each method call on an extern is resolved by name, as a string.
// E.g. calling foo() on an ExtPtr<Terrain> is the same as calling call("foo") on the underlying Node*.

// The @deferred attribute (allowed only in externs) makes calls go through call_deferred instead of call.

// In externs, @rpc takes no arguments: the class that defines the function configures it.
// Call it as in classes, e.g. `rpc my_extern->qux();`.

// With externs, a package can depend at runtime on other GD++ packages, or even on non-GD++ GDExtensions.
// But externs are slow by design. Use them as little as possible.
